[Codex (products & libraries)]: - There may be a model naming mismatch: I found current deployment material for **Gemma 3 27B**, not “Gemma 4 26B.” Gemma 3 has 1B/4B/12B/27B variants, with 4B/12B/27B supporting 128k context and multimodal input. Source: Hugging Face Gemma 3 notes: https://huggingface.co/blog/gemma3

- Product category: this sits between **AI contract review**, **CLM**, **legal RAG**, and **multi-agent legal QA**. The strongest commercial comparables are Harvey, Kira, Ironclad, Luminance, LegalOn, Spellbook, Robin AI, and LegalSifter.

- Harvey is the closest “north star” product: legal AI workspace, document storage, legal research, due diligence, contract analysis, and workflow agents. It emphasizes enterprise controls, source grounding, and task-specific legal workflows. Source: https://www.harvey.ai/

- Kira is the strongest comparison for extraction-heavy contract review and due diligence. It has built-in provision models, custom model training via Quick Study, folder/project review workflows, exports, source highlighting, and bulk contract analysis. Source: https://www.kira.ai/solutions/due-diligence/

- Ironclad is strong on CLM integration: intake, playbooks, clause detection, smart import, renewals, redlining, workflow automation, and contract data reuse. It is less just “contract review” and more contract lifecycle operations. Source: https://support.ironcladapp.com/hc/en-us/articles/12947738534935-Ironclad-AI-Overview

- Luminance is strong on enterprise contract intelligence: drafting, negotiation, non-standard clause detection, compliant alternatives, Word integration, portfolio insight, compliance trail, and institutional memory. Source: https://www.luminance.com/ai-contract-review-software/

- The gap in many leading tools: they are expensive, closed, enterprise-only, and hard to benchmark publicly. A student/research project can differentiate by making the pipeline inspectable: explicit roles, prompts, RAG evidence, model comparison, regression tests, dashboards, and failure cases.

- Open-source/research references worth studying:
  - **PAKTON**: open-source multi-agent contract QA with RAG, focused on long legal agreements and collaborative review. Very relevant to Harvey/Kira/Admin architecture. Source: https://openreview.net/forum?id=j7ewMuSQFY
  - **LawGlance**: open-source RAG legal assistant, though more general legal guidance than contract contradiction detection. Source: https://github.com/lawglance/lawglance
  - **CUAD**: expert-annotated contract review dataset, 510 contracts, 13k+ labels, 41 clause types. Best baseline dataset for clause/risk extraction. Source: https://www.atticusprojectai.org/cuad
  - **ContractNLI**: useful for contradiction/entailment/neutral reasoning on NDAs.
  - **LexGLUE / LEDGAR / UNFAIR-ToS**: useful legal NLP baselines.
  - **CLAUSE benchmark**: very relevant because it tests subtle legal discrepancies and contradictions. Source: https://clause-legal.github.io/

- RAG architecture should be scoped carefully:
  - Only **Harvey** gets RAG access.
  - Kira should inspect the uploaded contract itself for holes, ambiguity, missing clauses, weak drafting, and internal inconsistency.
  - Admin should merge Harvey + Kira outputs, resolve conflicts, and produce final findings with confidence, citations, and human-review flags.
  - The 3 verifier Harveys and 3 verifier Kiras should probably be independent reviewer passes with different prompt seeds/criteria, not separate product roles.

- RAG knowledge base should support two corpus types:
  - **Policies/playbooks**: organization standards, fallback clauses, approval rules, forbidden terms.
  - **Prior contracts/templates**: older agreements, standard forms, negotiated precedents.
  - Since current repo contracts are only training/sample data, the app should treat them as seed/demo corpus, not production tenant knowledge.

- Harvey contradiction checks should likely compare against:
  - policy family first,
  - then tenant-wide policy corpus,
  - then prior contracts/templates only when tagged as authoritative or precedent.
  - Comparing every uploaded contract against all older contracts will create noisy false positives unless documents are classified by type, jurisdiction, counterparty type, effective date, and authority level.

- Common libraries/APIs for this stack:
  - PDF/OCR parsing: Docling, Unstructured, PyMuPDF, Tesseract/EasyOCR, pdfplumber as fallback only.
  - Chunking/RAG: LlamaIndex, LangChain, Haystack, txtai, custom pipeline.
  - Vector DB: pgvector, Qdrant, Weaviate, Milvus, Chroma for local/demo.
  - Reranking: bge-reranker, Cohere Rerank, Jina reranker, ColBERT-style retrieval.
  - Inference: vLLM, Hugging Face TGI, SGLang, TensorRT-LLM, Ollama for local demos.
  - Evaluation: Ragas, DeepEval, Promptfoo, OpenAI Evals-style harness, custom legal rubric scoring.
  - Observability: MLflow/W&B for model experiments; Prometheus/Grafana for API/runtime metrics.

- Deployment platform notes:
  - For a live demo, use a managed GPU endpoint unless the rubric requires infra depth.
  - Koyeb has one-click Gemma 3 27B with vLLM, autoscaling, and scale-to-zero. Source: https://www.koyeb.com/deploy/gemma-3-27b
  - Hugging Face Inference Endpoints are good for model-hosting credibility and simple deployment.
  - RunPod, Modal, Lambda Labs, CoreWeave, Together AI, Fireworks AI are also plausible depending on budget and control needs.
  - Self-hosted vLLM on a rented H100/A100 gives best control for fine-tuned Gemma but adds DevOps risk.
  - For “fine-tuned Gemma + 3 non-fine-tuned models,” best architecture is separate model endpoint configs behind a common provider interface.

- Benchmark design to prove fine-tuned > non-fine-tuned:
  - Use same prompts, same retrieval context, same test set.
  - Compare base Gemma, instruction Gemma, fine-tuned Gemma, and maybe a strong hosted baseline.
  - Metrics: clause detection F1, contradiction detection precision/recall, citation accuracy, hallucination rate, severity calibration, reviewer agreement, latency, cost/request.
  - Datasets: CUAD for clause extraction, ContractNLI for contradiction/entailment, CLAUSE-style perturbations for subtle discrepancy detection, plus a small hand-labeled internal golden set.
  - Need thresholds like: fine-tuned model must improve contradiction F1 by X%, reduce hallucinations by Y%, or increase source-supported findings by Z%.

- Prompting should be versioned like code:
  - `prompts/harvey_v1.md`
  - `prompts/harvey_verifier_v1.md`
  - `prompts/kira_v1.md`
  - `prompts/kira_verifier_v1.md`
  - `prompts/admin_consensus_v1.md`
  - Include prompt changelog, evaluation score per version, and rollback path.

- Leading solution strengths:
  - Source-grounded answers.
  - Bulk document workflows.
  - Clause extraction with source spans.
  - Playbooks and company-specific negotiation positions.
  - Human review queues.
  - Audit logs and enterprise permissions.
  - Word/CLM integrations.
  - Dashboards and exports.

- Leading solution gaps your project can expose:
  - Little public benchmark transparency.
  - Hard to know when AI is wrong.
  - Limited explainability beyond citations.
  - Vendor lock-in.
  - Expensive enterprise pricing.
  - Weak reproducibility for model/prompt changes.
  - Often optimized for extraction, less for deep contradiction reasoning.

- Most important project artifacts to create later:
  - `DEPLOYMENT_RESEARCH.md`: platform comparison, GPU needs, cost notes, recommendation.
  - `UNTOUCHABLES_AND_LIMITATIONS.md`: what cannot be completed now, blocked dependencies, missing models/data/secrets.
  - `CHANGELOG_IMPLEMENTATION.md`: every change made and current completion level.
  - `EVALUATION_PLAN.md`: test suite, golden datasets, metrics, thresholds.
  - `ARCHITECTURE.md`: service boundaries, API contracts, Docker services, diagrams.
  - `PROMPT_REGISTRY.md`: prompt versions, role definitions, model selection logic.

- Product positioning: “AI legal review system for organizations with high contract volume, reducing outside counsel spend and review cycle time by combining contract-specific RAG, multi-agent review, human validation, and benchmarked model improvement.”

- Main risk: don’t overclaim legal correctness. Frame output as legal review assistance with cited risks, suggested questions, and human approval, not autonomous legal advice.
[Codex (technical patterns)]: **Current Repo Findings**
- Run-based backend is partly implemented now: `POST /api/runs`, `GET /api/runs/{id}`, SSE events, retry, human review, worker state machine, contract versions, workspaces, auth, and comments exist.
- Live product is still not rubric-complete: no Docker Compose, no Dockerfiles, no Kubernetes manifests, no CI, no public deployment link, no test suite, no monitoring dashboards, no benchmark harness, no RAG ingestion pipeline.
- Harvey currently loads `PolicyVersionRecord` JSON by `tenant_id + policy_family_id + version_number`; this is not true RAG over PDFs.
- Kira currently loads internal/external compliance corpora from DB JSON. User requirement says “Only Harvey will have access to RAG,” so Kira should not retrieve from contract/policy PDFs unless explicitly scoped as non-RAG compliance rules.
- Parser still uses `pdfplumber` first in `parser.py`, despite docs/requirements mentioning Docling. That is a rubric risk because document extraction quality and evidence anchoring are central.
- Frontend is connected to backend polling, but not SSE-driven. `App.tsx` still uses `pollRunUntilDone`; rubric asks end-to-end behavior and logs, but SSE is not yet surfaced in UI.
- There are no repo tests visible from `rg --files`; rubric requires unit/integration/E2E, golden datasets, regression checks, LLM eval logic, thresholds, and CI logs.

**Prompting Findings**
- Current reviewer prompts are too generic for legal-grade output. They say “surface issues” but do not define Harvey vs Kira sharply enough.
- Harvey prompt should explicitly check uploaded contract clauses against retrieved prior/current policy/contract passages and require contradiction pairs: `uploaded_clause`, `retrieved_policy_or_contract`, `conflict_type`, `source_doc_id`, `page`, `confidence`.
- Kira prompt should focus on holes inside the uploaded contract itself: ambiguity, missing fallback terms, undefined obligations, exploitability, missing remedies, contradictory clauses within the same uploaded contract.
- Admin is deterministic merge logic, not a true reasoning consensus layer. That may be acceptable for demo reliability, but the rubric’s “model/pipeline logic” would be stronger with an auditable consensus object.
- Final reviewer currently returns `branch="harvey"` in `FinalReviewerAgent`; that is misleading provenance.
- Prompt version tracking is missing. Add prompt files or DB/version constants: `harvey_v1`, `kira_v1`, `admin_v1`, `validator_v1`, with changelog and eval scores.

**RAG Architecture**
- Best practical stack: Docling/PyMuPDF extraction → chunk by clause/section → embeddings → vector DB → reranker → Harvey context pack → cited structured output.
- Good vector DB options:
  - Postgres + `pgvector`: simplest because app already uses Postgres; good for demo and moderate tenant scale.
  - Qdrant: better standalone vector service, filtering, payload metadata, clean Docker story.
  - Weaviate/Pinecone: useful later, but overkill for current local/demo scope.
- Use hybrid retrieval, not vector-only: BM25/full-text catches exact policy wording; embeddings catch semantic conflicts.
- Required RAG metadata: `tenant_id`, `doc_type`, `policy_family_id`, `contract_id`, `version`, `effective_from`, `effective_to`, `jurisdiction`, `source_path`, `page`, `clause_uid`, `chunk_hash`.
- Conflict checking decision needed: same policy family only is safer and cheaper; all tenant policies catches more contradictions but increases false positives and retrieval noise. For phase 1, use same family + explicitly linked playbooks, then add all-tenant optional search.

**Deployment Findings**
- Gemma 4 26B A4B exists as `google/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it`; model card says 25.2B total params, 3.8B active params, 256K context, Apache 2.0 license.
- Best platform for the requested setup is likely Modal: it has a current official Gemma 4 + vLLM example, OpenAI-compatible endpoint, H200 guidance, model/cache volumes, public URL, and faster iteration than Kubernetes.
- Second choice: Runpod Serverless vLLM. Strong for cost and scale-to-zero, but more worker/container ceremony.
- Hugging Face Inference Endpoints is clean for managed demos, autoscaling, and monitoring, but can be expensive and less flexible for custom multi-agent routing.
- Koyeb GPU can work for simpler vLLM service deployment with scale-to-zero, but I would not choose it first for four Gemma 4 26B-class models.
- Suggested demo architecture: Vercel/Netlify frontend, Render/Fly/Modal CPU FastAPI API, Neon/Supabase Postgres + pgvector, Modal vLLM endpoints for 1 fine-tuned Harvey and 3 non-fine-tuned verifier models.

Sources:
- Gemma 4 model card: https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it
- Modal Gemma + vLLM: https://modal.com/docs/examples/llm_inference
- Runpod vLLM serverless: https://docs.runpod.io/serverless/vllm/overview
- Hugging Face endpoint autoscaling: https://huggingface.co/docs/inference-endpoints/main/en/guides/autoscaling
- vLLM OpenAI server options: https://docs.vllm.ai/en/v0.8.3/serving/openai_compatible_server.html

**Evaluation Suite Needed**
- Build a fixed golden benchmark with contracts/policies withheld from training.
- Compare fine-tuned Harvey vs base Gemma 4 26B using identical prompts, retrieval context, decoding params, and JSON schema.
- Metrics needed:
  - JSON parse rate
  - schema completeness
  - contradiction recall
  - contradiction precision
  - finding F1/F2
  - evidence citation accuracy
  - severity calibration
  - hallucinated citation rate
  - latency and cost per contract
- Use paired evaluation per document/clause, not aggregate vibes.
- Statistical proof: bootstrap confidence intervals for F1 deltas; McNemar test for paired classification wins/losses.
- Minimum credible claim: “fine-tuned beats base by X points on contradiction F1 and reduces hallucinated citations by Y% on N held-out examples.”

**Docs To Create**
- `docs/CANNOT_TOUCH_YET.md`: no real fine-tuned model weights, no production legal corpus, no public endpoint credentials, no deployment account secrets, no live benchmark numbers until eval runs.
- `docs/CHANGES_MADE.md`: every code/doc/config change, current completion percentage, remaining rubric gaps.
- `docs/DEPLOYMENT_RESEARCH.md`: Modal-first recommendation, Runpod/HF/Koyeb comparison, GPU/cost assumptions, architecture diagram.
- `docs/RUBRIC_GAP_ANALYSIS.md`: rubric item → current evidence → missing work → priority.
- `docs/EVALUATION_PLAN.md`: benchmark design, datasets, metrics, thresholds, experiment table.
- `docs/RAG_DESIGN.md`: Harvey-only RAG ingestion, retrieval, metadata, contradiction workflow.

**Highest-Risk Gaps**
- No tests or CI.
- No true RAG.
- No public deployment.
- No benchmark results proving fine-tuning improves anything.
- No Docker/service deployment boundary.
- Prompting is not yet strong enough for legal contradiction detection with evidence.
- Current code can demo a pipeline, but not yet satisfy the full rubric defensibly.
